  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Dotted Stem Bolete (Neoboletus erythropus) is a striking fungus with a reddish-orange cap and bright yellow pores that stain blue when bruised. It is a choice edible mushroom in Europe, though it requires proper preparation and cooking to remove toxins.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Blue Staining Reaction",
      "description": "Pores and flesh turn bright blue when cut or bruised, a distinctive diagnostic feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap and Stem Coloring",
      "description": "Reddish-orange to brick-red cap with a yellow stem that may have reddish dots or streaks",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "Bright yellow to orange pores beneath the cap that are angular and run slightly down the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, often near oaks and conifers from late summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being toxic when raw, this bolete is prized in European cuisine after proper cooking, which neutralizes the poisonous compounds—a practice less common in North America where similar-looking toxic species create confusion.",
